\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - Asia  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  off highs as Sing dlr fizzles. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 755 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Februar 1998\par 12:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Feb 27 (Reuters) - Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained firm but were off their highs in late Friday trade as the Singapore dollar subsided after the country's 1998 budget failled to deliver any major tax cuts. \par \par \uc2  But traders were reluctant to commit themselves aggressively due to possible weekend developments, including an ASEAN finance ministers meeting in Jakarta expected to address the proposal of using regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for intra-ASEAN trade. \par \par \uc2  "We could see totally different levels when we come in on Monday so I don't think people want to carry positions over the weekend," a U.S. bank dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar's decline against the yen and gains in several Asian stock markets following Wall Street's bullish performance helped regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  stay on positive ground. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar slipped back through the 1.62 level to the U.S. dollar in thin overseas markets as Finance Minister Richard Hu concluded his budget speech without any mention of a widely-sought corporate tax cut. \par \par \uc2  Hu announced a series of tax concessions for the financial sector, but added that Singapore's tax rates remained competitive and no major tax changes were to be made against a backdrop of lower revenues and rising development expenditure. \par \par \uc2  He said Singapore should see a budget surplus of S$2.7 billion, or 1.7 percent of gross domestic product (GDP) in 1998, compared with an average surplus of 3.8 percent of GDP in 1996 and 1997. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar reached early highs of 1.6100/10 to the U.S. dollar on stop-loss U.S. dollar sales sparked by expectations of a pro-business budget. \par \par \uc2  It was also lifted by Deputy Prime Minister Lee Hsien Loong's comment that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS) would maintain confidence in the domestic dollar by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets when necessary. \par \par \uc2  Lee told parliament fears of a sharp fall in the rupiah dragging down the Singapore dollar were hypothetical at this stage and "the weight of the rupiah in our basket is small". \par \par \uc2  Talk of MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar from strengthening through the 1.61 level in early trade, but dealers said it was likely to target the 1.60 level soon. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit tracked its neighbour's moves, benefitting further from gains in Kuala Lumpur stocks and news the government was raising the ceiling on foreign share ownership in telecommunications companies. \par \par \uc2  News Standard and Poor's Corp had lowered its ratings on six Malaysian banks failed to dampen sentiment, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah rose through the 9,000 per dollar level on U.S. dollar sales by U.S. investment houses in the wake of the Singapore dollar's early rise. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said worries about unrest ahead of the presidential elections and uncertainty over Indonesia's plans to stabilise its rupiah via a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system would limit gains to the 8,500 level. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht made way for investor and corporate dollar demand after failing to firm above the 42.50 per dollar level. Dealers said the market was largely unmoved by economic data showing a $1.03 billion trade surplus and a $1.18 billion current account surplus for December. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso firmed with its regional counterparts and as foreign funds flowed into stock and fixed income markets. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it was planning to cut banks' reserve requirements by three percentage points and raise liquidity reserves by the same amount to nudge bank lending rates lower. \par \par \uc2  In north Asia, the Taiwan dollar ended firm on the yen's strength and confidence over the appointment of new \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Perng Nai-fan, known for his tough stance against speculators. \par \par \uc2  Perng said after his swearing-in on Thursday that he favoured "dynamic stability" in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market but would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the face of abnormal factors -- echoing his predecessor Sheu Yuan-dong's stance. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won finished above the psychological 1,645 level to the dollar on month-end export deals and a surging stock market. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar was firm while forwards rebounded after the Hong Kong banks' association left deposit rates unchanged at its weekly review meeting. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) was heard offering longer-dated funds on Friday, though it said it had not been interfering in money markets to add liquidity and push down interest rates. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926du2r00zmo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
