\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Asia  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  cool to Indonesia debt halt. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 803 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Januar 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Jan 27 (Reuters) - Indonesia's proposal of a voluntary freeze on corporate debt servicing caused few ripples in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on Tuesday as most banks had already wound down for Lunar New Year and Moslem Eid al-Fitr holidays. \par \par \uc2  The freeze on foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt servicing, effective immediately, would allow time for borrowers and lenders to work out new arrangements to restore debt payments as soon as possible, said Radius Prawiro, appointed by Indonesian President Suharto to oversee the debt crisis. \par \par \uc2  Prawiro said a steering committee of international bankers from Indonesia's main creditor countries, led by senior officials from Standard Chartered Bank and UBS, and a committee of borrowers would form a framework to manage the massive private sector debt. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the move had been widely expected by the market as some Indonesian companies had already stopped servicing their debts following the rupiah's 80 percent drop in value against the U.S. dollar since July. \par \par \uc2  "It is a necessary measure that provides some breathing space for the Indonesian economy. In the long-term, it is a step in the right direction," said Andy Tan, general manager of MMS/Standard and Poor's in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "In the short term, it could cause more confusion and will probably create some selling pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But a lot depends on the details," he added. \par \par \uc2  But the rupiah showed little reaction to the news, edging up to 12,000/12,100 per dollar against 12,500/13,500 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  "This would amount to a moratorium, which is what's been feared. That's why we stopped trading the rupiah. Nobody's touching it at all today," said a senior regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "The whole day's been very quiet. It's ranged between 12,100 and 13,500 and not much has been dealt within that," he said. \par \par \uc2  Some \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said the Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the rupiah in the face of persistent dollar demand in a thin market. \par \par \uc2  But any gains were expected to be short lived. \par \par \uc2  "Even if there's joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the holidays, we'll only see a temporary correction in the rupiah. A lot still needs to be done and it will take a long time before people get back their confidence," a U.S. bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Jakarta dealers said the rupiah had strengthened briefly after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised rates on its certificates for all maturities and announced reforms to the banking sector. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Malaysian ringgit and Singapore dollar gained ground as Indonesia's announcement prompted players to unload some of their long dollar positions. \par \par \uc2  "In the event of a moratorium, there'll be no need for dollar buying against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . So it's a speculative move, those guys who are long are just throwing back (their dollars)," a U.S. bank dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit firmed to 4.3850/950 per dollar at 0520 GMT from 4.5300/600 late on Monday. The Singapore dollar rose to 1.7335/85 against 1.7535/65 earlier. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht strengthened to 54.40/55 per dollar onshore against 54.70/90 late on Monday as exporters unloaded dollars in a thin market. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said trading was subdued due to holidays elsewhere in the region, although Thai markets are not closed for the Lunar New Year. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso was quoted at 43.00/60 to the dollar against Monday's 43.08 close as dollar demand dwindled ahead of holidays in the region. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its call money rate -- a benchmark for interbank call loan trading -- to 14 percent from 12 percent. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar slipped to HK$7.7445/65 to the U.S. dollar from Monday's 7.7438/58 close, but it was expected to find support at 7.7480 despite soft interbank rates. \par \par \uc2  The Indian rupee firmed to 38.420/450 to the dollar against a previous 38.70/73 close on exporter dollar sales after a long weekend. \par \par \uc2  Markets in Taiwan and South Korea were closed for the Lunar New Year. \par \par \uc2  The following table shows the drop in value of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the crisis began in July. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements are in percentage terms and reflect the local unit's fall against the dollar, not the dollar's rise. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \tab    Current value    Move since July 1.\par  Indonesian Rupiah     11,500.00\tab     -78.85%\par  Thai Baht\tab \tab      54.40\tab     -52.39%\par  Korean Won\tab \tab  1,675.00\tab     -46.99%\par  Malaysian Ringgit\tab     4.41\tab     -42.86%\par  Philippine Peso\tab      43.00\tab     -38.37%\par  Taiwan dollar\tab \tab  33.75\tab     -17.78%\par  Singapore dollar\tab      1.73\tab     -17.51%\par  Hong Kong dollar\tab      7.74\tab \tab 0.01%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (c) Reuters Limited 1998\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du1r015rj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\