\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  wind down for holidays. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 760 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Januar 1998\par 10:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Jan 26 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded sluggishly on Monday as activity dwindled to position adjustment ahead of the Lunar New Year and Moslem Eid al-Fitr festivities throughout the region. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's loss of face against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to mounting concern about a sex scandal involving U.S. President Bill Clinton also gave bruised Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  some relief. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is quite well offered at the moment. It looks like Clinton's helping the Far East \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  a little bit," a senior regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar briefly fell below 125 yen in early trade and extended its losses against the mark and Swiss franc amid allegations of misconduct by Clinton and a British newspaper report that the G7 nations were likely to agree next month to boost the yen. \par \par \uc2  Even the Indonesian rupiah, which has led the fray in recent sessions, showed signs of stability, though dealers said its undertone remained weak due to the unresolved issue of massive corporate debt. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah was a shade firmer at 12,250/13,250 to the dollar at 0840 GMT against an opening at 13,500/14,500. \par \par \uc2  Some \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said Bank Indonesia was seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market around midday, pushing the rupiah to a high of 13,000 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  But trade was very subdued, with many players reluctant to take positions after key Japanese Finance Ministry official Eisuke Sakakibara said on Sunday Japan planned some measures with the IMF this week on Indonesia's financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  Concerns over mounting private debt and political problems have haunted the market and sent the rupiah to a historic low of 17,000 last week. \par \par \uc2  "The market's been more or less stable today because no one dared to do anything ... Japan didn't give any details, which makes the market a bit nervous," a U.S. bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said trading was also hampered by ambiguity over the maturity of the spot date due to the impending Eid al-Fitr holiday, which ends the Moslem fasting month of Ramadan. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht was at 54.65/75 to the dollar onshore against 54.60/75 late on Friday as the market awaited details of talks between Thai Finance Minister Tarrin Nimmanahaeminda and the IMF on a possible review of Bangkok's bail-out terms. \par \par \uc2  Tarrin said on Friday the IMF and Thailand were in broad agreement on technical changes to the programme to make it more flexible, but he declined to detail the changes. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit whipped around in a thin market, receiving a slight boost from the dollar's weakened stance. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar sagged to 1.7570/1.7620 to the U.S. dollar from 1.7515/65 four hours earlier. Dealers said it was likely to keep to a 1.7450-1.7650 range in the short term. \par \par \uc2  The market shrugged off comments by Deputy Prime Minister Lee Hsien Loong on Sunday that, barring shocks, Singapore was not expected to slide into a recession. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso recovered from a weak start as corporate demand for the dollar subsided and the market absorbed Friday's news of a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  plan to increase dollar liquidity by entering into swaps with banks. \par \par \uc2  In north Asia, the Korean won ended at 1,688 to the dollar from Friday's 1,745 close on exporter dollar sales and hopes of a speedy conclusion to Korean debt talks in New York. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Lim Chang-yuel said talks with international creditors were likely to be concluded in February. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar shed early gains to importer dollar demand and finished at T$33.996 against a previous 33.925 close. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar dipped to HK$7.7443/63 per U.S. dollar from Friday's 7.7400/20 close and forwards firmed amid jitters about insolvencies at local brokerage firms. \par \par \uc2  The following table shows the drop in value of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the crisis began in July. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements are in percentage terms and reflect the local unit's fall against the dollar, not the dollar's rise. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \tab    Current value    Move since July 1.\par  Indonesian Rupiah     12,250.00\tab     -80.15%\par  Thai Baht\tab \tab      54.65\tab     -52.61%\par  Korean Won\tab \tab  1,675.00\tab     -46.99%\par  Malaysian Ringgit\tab     4.53\tab     -44.37%\par  Philippine Peso\tab      43.05\tab     -38.44%\par  Taiwan dollar\tab \tab  34.00\tab     -18.37%\par  Singapore dollar\tab      1.76\tab     -18.73%\par  Hong Kong dollar\tab      7.74\tab \tab 0.02%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (c) Reuters Limited 1998\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du1q017ap\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
