\par\par \b \uc2  Chronology of the Asian financial crisis. \b0\par\par\uc2 834 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 1998\par 05:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Jan 15 (Reuters) - President Suharto of Indonesia and the International Monetary Fund (IMF) Managing Director, Michel Camdessus, signed an agreement on Thursday to strengthen economic reforms. \par \par \uc2  The following is a chronology of the financial crisis that has rocked Asia. \par \par \uc2  1997 \par \par \uc2  Jan 23 - Leading South Korean steel maker Hanbo Steel Corp defaults on loans, the first of a string of major corporate failures in 1997. \par \par \uc2  May 14-15 - Thailand's baht \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  comes under attack by speculators who decided Thailand's slowing economy and political instability meant it was time to sell. Thailand and \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the baht. \par \par \uc2  The Philippines is affected. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raises the overnight rate 1-3/4 percentage points to 13 percent and dumps dollars. \par \par \uc2  June 19 - Amnuay Viravan, staunchly against devaluing the baht, resigns as Thai finance minister. Resignation has immediate impact in the Philippines, where the overnight deposit rate rises to 15 percent. \par \par \uc2  June 27 - Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspends \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  of 16 cash-strapped finance companies and orders them to submit merger or consolidation plans. \par \par \uc2  June 30 - Thai Prime Minister Chavalit Yonchaiyudh assures the nation in a televised address there will be no devaluation of the baht. \par \par \uc2  July 2 - The Bank of Thailand announces a managed float of the baht and calls on the International Monetary Fund for "technical assistance". \par \par \uc2  The announcement effectively devalues the baht by about 15-20 percent. It ends at a record low of 28.80 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  In Manila, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is forced to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to defend the peso. \par \par \uc2  July 8 - Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Negara has to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively to defend the ringgit. \par \par \uc2  In Indonesia, the rupiah starts to crumble. Jakarta widens its rupiah trading band to 12 from eight percent. \par \par \uc2  July 14 - The IMF offers the Philippines almost $1.1 billion in financial support under fast-track regulations drawn up after the 1995 Mexican crisis. \par \par \uc2  July 24 - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  meltdown. The rupiah, baht, ringgit and peso all slump, as confidence in the region rapidly deteriorates. Around this time the Singapore dollar starts a gradual decline. \par \par \uc2  August 11 - In Tokyo the IMF unveils a rescue package for Thailand including loans of $16 billion from the IMF and Asian nations. \par \par \uc2  August 14 - Indonesia abolishes its system of a managed exchange rate. The rupiah plunges. \par \par \uc2  August 20 - IMF approves a $3.9 billion credit for Thailand. Brunei later adds $500 million to the bailout package, bringing it to total of $17.2 billion. \par \par \uc2  Sept 20 - Malaysian Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad tells delegates to IMF/World Bank annual conference in Hong Kong that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading is immoral and should be stopped. \par \par \uc2  Oct 6 - Rupiah hits a low of 3,845. \par \par \uc2  Oct 6 - Russia and London Club sign agreement rescheduling roughly $33 billion of debts over 25 years with seven years grace, dated from December 1995. \par \par \uc2  Oct 8 - Indonesia says it will ask the IMF for financial assistance. \par \par \uc2  Oct 20-23 - The Hong Kong stock market suffers its heaviest drubbing ever, shedding nearly a quarter of its value in four days on uncertainty over the Hong Kong dollar. The Hang Seng index plunges 23.34 percent to 10,426.30 by Thursday's close, after 13,601.01 the previous Friday. \par \par \uc2  Around this time the South Korean won also begins to slump rapidly in value. \par \par \uc2  Oct 31 - Indonesia's IMF package is unveiled. It provides for as much as $40 billion in aid, although the front-line defence is $23 billion. \par \par \uc2  Nov 5 - IMF approves a $10 billion loan for Indonesia as part of the massive international package. \par \par \uc2  Dec 4 - IMF approves a $21 billion loan for South Korea. Two days later the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Korea announces that it received $5.22 billion. \par \par \uc2  1998 \par \par \uc2  Jan 6 - Indonesia unveils its 1998/99 budget projecting an increase of 32.1 percent growth in revenue and expenditure over the current budget and a four percent economic growth rate. \par \par \uc2  But the rupiah loses half its value against the dollar over five-day period, breaking through the 10,000 to the dollar level, before a slight recovery. \par \par \uc2  Freefall is triggered by perceptions that budget not tough enough to meet IMF-mandated austerity measures and by market talk that Jakarta might declare debt moratorium. \par \par \uc2  Jan 8 - Representatives of the IMF and South Korea agree to a 90-day rollover of short-term debt. \par \par \uc2  Jan 10 - Indonesia's President Suharto comes under increasing international pressure to adhere to IMF conditions. He calls the delay or review of 15 big infrastructure projects. \par \par \uc2  Jan 11 - The IMF's first Deputy Managing Director Stanley Fischer arrives in Jakarta for talks about the IMF's programme and how to move ahead with it. \par \par \uc2  Jan 14 - IMF Managing Director Camdessus heads for Jakarta. Stopping off in Singapore he says the IMF is asking Indonesia to sign a new letter of intent committing itself to implementing further IMF reforms. \par \par \uc2  Jan 15 - Camdessus and President Suharto sign an agreement  strengthening economic reforms. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du1f06l5p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
