\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  hunker down for Indonesia. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Phil Smith \par \uc2 903 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 1998\par 11:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Jan 12 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  sat relatively tight during a moribund session as the market awaited the outcome of what is being seen as make or break talks between the International Monetary Fund (IMF) and Indonesia. \par \par \uc2  Towards the end of last week analysts and traders had been looking seriously at the chance of Indonesia calling a debt moratorium but most are now cautiously confident this week's talks will yield some solid action and see economic and banking sector reforms finally put in place. \par \par \uc2  The bloodied rupiah was basically steady around the 8,400 per dollar level against the opening 8,800 as traders opted to stay out amid widening spreads. Although this was weaker than Friday's 8,000 close it was well above last Thursday's dive below 10,000. \par \par \uc2  President Suharto on Monday met IMF Deputy Managing Director Stanley Fischer and will on Wednesday have talks with Managing Director Michel Camdessus. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers was due in Jakarta late on Monday to add weight to international pressure for Indonesia to reform. \par \par \uc2  The IMF said Monday's meetings with Suharto were "very, very good." \par \par \uc2  "Early indications are that talks are progressing well," said Thio Chin Loo, strategist at Banque Paribas in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "Although the outlook for the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  would hinge on developments in Indonesia/IMF/U.S. talks, a debt moratorium in Indonesia has been averted for now as well as capital controls and a fixed exchange rate regime." \par \par \uc2  While a number of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were  steady, the Thai baht plumbed new depths, sinking below 55.00 per dollar with traders unimpressed by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's measures to curb \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculation. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Thailand said late on Friday it would step up scrutiny of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and threatened to impose severe penalties for trading violations. \par \par \uc2  "There's been unwinding of long rupiah/baht positions. And the baht has also weakened after Friday's capital controls because not much action was taken," the Singapore dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The baht was at 56.20 to the dollar onshore against 54.00 late on Friday with the offshore rate at 56.00 against 53.00. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit was softer at 4.6700 per dollar from 4.60 late on Friday, but dealers said its decline was being capped by dollar sales from U.S. funds above 4.70. \par \par \uc2  They said activity was subdued as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stayed out after three straight days of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar slipped to 1.7980 to the U.S. dollar from 1.7700 late Friday as domestic interbank rates eased. Overnight rate fell below 10 percent from highs of 36 percent last week, spurring speculation the authorities had added liquidity to the system in an effort to shore up the sagging stock market. \par \par \uc2  But any extra liquidity did little for the Straits Times Industrials index which closed 8.75 percent lower after dropping 23 percent last week. \par \par \uc2  "The stock market's been battered for the last three sessions. If it goes on like this, there'll be a lot of defaults and bank shares especially will be affected," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "I think the (\b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore) is monitoring the situation closely and will continue to put back liquidity to ease the crunch," he said. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar was steady around Friday's 7.7430 close, but local interest rates and forwards rose sharply. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market appeared to be gearing for another attack on the Hong Kong dollar peg as the territory's stock market came under intense pressure. \par \par \uc2  The Hang Seng Index lost over 11.0 percent at one stage after Peregrine Investments, one of Asia's largest independent investment banks, went into liquidation. \par \par \uc2  "If the stock market continues to plunge, the pressure will be high to lift the peg," a dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  "If they're able to hold it for six months and the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  stabilise, the speculators will get badly burned because of the carry costs," he said. \par \par \uc2  "But the funds are very much enriched by the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  fall, so they have a lot of firepower. It will be interesting to see whether the Hong Kong dollar can hold up." \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the South Korean won firmed to 1,735 per U.S. dollar from Friday's 1,810 close on foreign fund inflows to the stock market, and the Taiwan dollar weakened to T$34.480 per U.S. dollar against a previous T$34.100 close as the yen shed some gains. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso traded sluggishly at 43.650 to the dollar against Friday's 44.30 close. \par \par \uc2  The following table shows the drop in value of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the crisis began in July. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements are in percentage terms and reflect the local unit's fall against the dollar, not the dollar's rise. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \tab    Current value    Move since July 1.\par  Indonesian Rupiah\tab 8,300.00\tab     -70.70%\par  Thai Baht\tab \tab      55.85\tab     -56.13%\par  Korean Won\tab \tab  1,735.00\tab     -48.82%\par  Malaysian Ringgit\tab     4.66\tab     -45.86%\par  Philippine Peso\tab      43.65\tab     -39.29%\par  Taiwan dollar\tab \tab     34.48\tab     -19.52%\par  Singapore dollar\tab \tab   1.80\tab     -20.60%\par  Hong Kong dollar\tab \tab   7.74\tab     -0.01%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -- Singapore newsroom (65) 870-3080 Fax (65) 776-8112\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -- Email: singapore.newsroom@reuters.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (c) Reuters Limited 1998\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du1c09bm3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
