\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  bide time for Indonesia. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 1,059 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 1998\par 06:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Jan 12 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  bided their time on Monday as traders nervously looked for signs that Indonesia planned to deal more effectively with its financial crisis after last week's confidence-shaking budget. \par \par \uc2  Most \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets withstood the waves of selling in stock markets across Asia as dealers awaited the outcome of talks between Indonesian leaders and IMF officials and the arrival of a U.S. team led by Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers. \par \par \uc2  But there was little optimism. \par \par \uc2  "These visits have created some uncertainty, so the market is just awaiting developments out of Indonesia. But I think the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will resume their weakening after the visits," the head of trading for regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a European bank in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah was practically static around 8,300/8800 to the dollar at 0525 GMT against its opening 8,700/900 as traders opted to stay out amid widening spreads. \par \par \uc2  "There is barely a trade going on. It's dangerously slow. People are just waiting for the outcome of the IMF meeting," said a Japanese bank dealer in Jakarta. \par \par \uc2  International Monetary Fund First Deputy Managing Director described his meeting with Suharto as encouraging, but provided no specific details. \par \par \uc2  Indonesian Finance Minister Mar'ie Muhammad said the outcome of the IMF talks would be announced on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Jakarta stocks rose nearly three percent as spirits improved with the IMF talks signalling the start of an international effort to persuade Jakarta not to backtrack on its commitment to reforms tied to a $43 billion IMF-led bailout. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market was also awaiting Summers' arrival in Jakarta on Monday, where he was expected to urge Indonesian leaders to push ahead with the reform process. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere in Southeast Asia, the Thai baht ploughed new depths, crumbling through the 55.00 per dollar level onshore as players remained unimpressed by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's measures to curb \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculation. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Thailand said late on Friday it would step up scrutiny of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and threatened to impose severe penalties on violators. \par \par \uc2  "There's been unwinding of long rupiah/baht positions. And the baht has also weakened after Friday's capital controls because not much action was taken," the Singapore dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The baht was at 55.30/60 to the dollar onshore against 53.85/54.15 late on Friday. Offshore rate at 54.20/70 against 52.50/53.50. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit was soft at 4.6000/50 per dollar from 4.5700/4.6200 late on Friday, but dealers said its decline was being capped by dollar sales from U.S. funds above 4.70. \par \par \uc2  They said activity was subdued as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stayed out after three straight days of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar slipped to 1.7840/90 to the U.S. dollar from 1.7690/7740 late on Friday as domestic interbank rates softened. \par \par \uc2  The overnight rate fell back below 10 percent from highs of 36 percent last week, spurring speculation the de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had added liquidity to the system in an effort to shore up the sagging stock market. \par \par \uc2  But the benchmark Straits Times Industrials index remained downcast, shedding 8.90 percent to 1,071.66 at the midday break. \par \par \uc2  "The stock market's been battered for the last three sessions. If it goes on like this, there'll be a lot of defaults and bank shares especially will be affected," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "I think the (\b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore) is monitoring the situation closely and will continue to put back liquidity to ease the crunch," he said. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso traded sluggishly at 44.05/20 to the dollar against Friday's 44.30 close. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar edged up to 7.7405/25 per U.S. dollar from Friday's 7.7430 close, while local interest rates and forwards rose sharply. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market appeared to be gearing for another attack on the Hong Kong dollar peg as the territory's stock market came under intense pressure. \par \par \uc2  The blue chip Hang Seng Index lost 11.08 percent to 7,909.13 points by the mid-session close on concerns about the fate of troubled Peregrine investment bank and following Friday's prime rate rise. \par \par \uc2  "If the stock market continues to plunge, the pressure will be high to lift the peg," a dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  "If they're able to hold it for six months and the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  stabilise, the speculators will get badly burned because of the carry costs," he said. \par \par \uc2  "But the funds are very much enriched by the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  fall, so they have a lot of firepower. It will be interesting to see whether the Hong Kong dollar can hold up." \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won firmed to 1,725/30 per U.S. dollar from Friday's 1,810 close on foreign fund inflows to the stock market, but players remained nervous about developments in Indonesia. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Lim Chang-yuel quoted IMF Managing Director Michel Camdessus as saying South Korea's financial situation had been improving since November. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar weakened to T$34.346/389 per U.S. dollar against a previous T$34.100 close as the yen shed some gains. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar clambered above US$0.64 from Friday's 0.6399/04 close, but players kept a wary eye on Southeast Asian markets. \par \par \uc2  The New Zealand dollar shed its early gains, inspired by the Aussie's bounce, to stand at US$0.5686/93 against an 0.5694/01 close on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The following table shows the drop in value of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the crisis began in July. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements are in percentage terms and reflect the local unit's fall against the dollar, not the dollar's rise. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \tab    Current value    Move since July 1.\par  Indonesian Rupiah\tab 8,300.00\tab     -70.70%\par  Thai Baht\tab \tab      55.30\tab     -53.16%\par  Korean Won\tab \tab  1,726.00\tab     -48.55%\par  Malaysian Ringgit\tab     4.58\tab     -44.98%\par  Philippine Peso\tab      44.05\tab     -39.84%\par  Taiwan dollar\tab \tab     34.35\tab     -19.20%\par  Singapore dollar\tab \tab   1.78\tab     -19.84%\par  Hong Kong dollar\tab \tab   7.74\tab     -0.06%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -- Singapore newsroom (65) 870-3080 Fax (65) 776-8112\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -- Email: singapore.newsroom@reuters.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (c) Reuters Limited 1998\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du1c09bcb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
