\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  traders frozen with fear. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 1,037 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 1998\par 06:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Jan 9 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading was practically petrified on Friday as activity shrank amid mounting panic about the potential outcome of Indonesia's financial debacle. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said liquidity had dwindled in Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as spreads widened amid fears that Indonesia was on the verge of declaring a debt moratorium or introducing trading curbs on the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost nearly half its value against the U.S. dollar in the last six trading days. \par \par \uc2  "There's not much trading going on. Everything is customer- or commercially-driven at the moment," said a U.S. bank dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  "In the rupiah, the interbank market doesn't exist anymore. Yesterday, the spread was about 100 rupiah. Today, it's 500-1,000, so it's practically impossible to trade," he said. \par \par \uc2  Financial markets were rife with rumours about street demonstrations in Jakarta and the health of President Suharto. Both were denied by military and government officials. \par \par \uc2  There was also talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the U.S. Federal Reserve to support the rupiah overnight and the immediate release of the second $3.0 billion tranche of IMF loans to Indonesia. But neither could be confirmed. \par \par \uc2  "These are all baseless rumours. The only true one was that there was a rush on basic commodities. I think Singapore players have built up extensive long dollar positions," a U.S. bank dealer in Jakarta said. \par \par \uc2  Dollar sales by state banks in Jakarta and follow-through selling by Singapore operators helped the rupiah rebound more than 30 percent to 7,900 per dollar from its opening around 10,500/11,500. \par \par \uc2  News that U.S. Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers would soon visit Indonesia and other Asian countries also helped calm Indonesian markets. \par \par \uc2  Summers, who has called on Jakarta to step up efforts to convince markets it is committed to IMF-sponsored economic reforms, would travel to the region to consult on the economic situation, said White House press secretary Mike McCurry. \par \par \uc2  He said in a statement Clinton had spoken to Suharto and Singapore Prime Minister Goh Chok Tong by telephone and underscored the importance of the region to the United States. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Malaysian ringgit firmed to 4.6200/300 to the dollar from lows of around 4.7800, following \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and calming words by the country's leaders. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad and Finance Minister Anwar Ibrahim both said Malaysao would not seek help from the IMF. \par \par \uc2  Mahathir said Kuala Lumpur had sufficient reserves to defend the ringgit and that depositor funds were safe. \par \par \uc2  Malaysia also announced a trade surplus of 700 million ringgit for November compared with a 500 million ringgit deficit the year before. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said the ringgit would remain under pressure from corporate demand for the dollar and weakness in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock markets. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar also showed a slightly healthier tone amid higher domestic interbank rates and talk of overnight \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS) had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in thin overseas markets to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar after it fell to a more than seven-year-low of 1.8160 to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  The MAS does not comment on its market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht recovered from record lows in onshore and offshore markets as traders turned cautious ahead of the announcement of new measures to curb speculation in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , expected later on Friday. \par \par \uc2  It was at 53.60/80 to the dollar onshore against 53.70/54.00 in early trade. The offshore rate was at 52.00/53.00 against 54.10/60 earlier. \par \par \uc2  But dealers remained sceptical about the potential benefits of the new measures and said that sentiment for the baht remained weak amid further credit ratings downgrades. \par \par \uc2  "We don't expect much really because it is quite difficult to limit the speculators. What we really need is foreign inflows. Also it takes time to do these things and you need laws and regulations. It doesn't just happen," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso firmed to 44.595 to the dollar against a previous 44.92 close on a market perception that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was providing dollar liquidity indirectly. \par \par \uc2  Police said they would shut down 130 money changers in Manila's main tourist district, accusing them of hoarding dollars and contributing to the peso's decline. \par \par \uc2  North Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were relatively shielded from the mayhem elsewhere, mainly due to the yen's relative strength against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar was at T$34.03/13 to the U.S. dollar against Thursday's T$34.325 close. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar was at HK$7.7435/55 against Thursday's HK$7.7455 close. Forwards also backed off a bit. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won was at 1,800/05 to the dollar against a low of 1,815 as the positive impact of news that global bankers had reaffirmed short-term financing for the country was countered by jitters about Indonesia. \par \par \uc2  The Austalian dollar was much higher at US$0.6404/09 from morning levels of around 0.6320/25, as a big buy order sparked rumours the Reserve Bank had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spokesman said the bank's policy was never to comment on market rumours or its \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The rumours also boosted the New Zealand dollar to US$0.5705/15 from an opening 0.5673/80. \par \par \uc2  The following table shows the drop in value of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the crisis began in July. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements are in percentage terms and reflect the local unit's fall against the dollar, not the dollar's rise. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \tab  Current value    Move since July 1.\par  Indonesian Rupiah\tab 8,500.00\tab     -71.39%\par  Thai Baht\tab \tab   53.60\tab \tab  -51.68%\par  Korean Won\tab \tab 1,805.00\tab      -50.80%\par  Malaysian Ringgit     4.65\tab \tab    -45.81%\par  Philippine Peso\tab  44.60\tab \tab   -40.58%\par  Taiwan dollar\tab \tab 34.03\tab \tab   -18.45%\par  Singapore dollar\tab    1.77\tab \tab    -19.05%\par  Hong Kong dollar\tab    7.74\tab \tab    -0.02%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -- Singapore newsroom (65) 870-3080 Fax (65) 776-8112\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -- Email: singapore.newsroom@reuters.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (c) Reuters Limited 1998\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du19098ur\par }\page {\par\fs2