\par\par \b \uc2  Monetary Officials Provide Respite For Malaysia, Singapore  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 762 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 1998\par 14:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Officials in Malaysia and Singapore burst a speculative bubble Wednesday, sending some Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  up against the U.S. dollar in late Asian trading. \par \par \uc2  But traders said that even as speculators retreated under the pressure from authorities, demand for dollars remains robust and regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are likely to remain under selling pressure. \par \par \uc2  "It doesn't take much to shake (speculators) out of the trees," a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer in Singapore said. The official who did most of the shaking was Malaysian Finance Minister Anwar Ibrahim, who said Malaysia's ringgit is "grossly undervalued in an environment of highly unstable market conditions." \par \par \uc2  Anwar confirmed that Bank Negara, the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Late in the Asian session, the ringgit, which traders said was a focus of speculative pressure, was almost 7% stronger against the U.S. dollar compared with its intraday low, and the Singapore dollar had gained more than 2%. \par \par \uc2  At about 6 a.m. EST, the U.S. dollar was trading at 4.5755 ringgit, down from a record 4.8800 ringgit earlier in the day, but up from 4.3400 ringgit late Tuesday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  Setting the stage for Anwar's remarks and Bank Negara's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or MAS, which serves as the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , warned that it "stands ready to act decisively against any speculative attack against the Singapore dollar." \par \par \uc2  The MAS's managing director, Koh Yong Guan, also reminded markets that the agency \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  "from time to time" in order to "ensure orderly movements" on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. The MAS emphasized that it manages the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  "and not against any single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  While the Singapore dollar has weakened against the U.S. dollar, the German mark and the British pound, the MAS said, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has risen against the ringgit, the Thai baht and the Indonesian rupiah. "Overall, on a trade-weighted basis, its value has remained stable, notwithstanding the (regional) \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  turmoil," the MAS said. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar didn't react immediately to Koh's comments, but traders said the remarks gave the market food for thought. The MAS "would carry more weight than the other central banks because it has the ability to act," a dealer in Singapore said. "But at the end of the day, I would think that their concern is not so much the right value for the Singapore dollar, but the speed at which it is depreciating." \par \par \uc2  In late trading, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at 1.7529 Singapore dollars, down from an intraday high of S$1.7930, its highest level since late March 1991, but above S$1.7375 late Tuesday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  Some traders said that with underlying demand for U.S. dollars still robust, Wednesday's scant show of strength isn't likely to be sustainable. "The tough thing for the central banks is that as soon as they take their foot off the gas, it will go back up again," one trader said of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Traders said the sharp round of weakening early in the Asian day was triggered by Indonesian President Suharto's budget message late Tuesday. Disappointment with Indonesia's 1998-99 budget sent the rupiah to a record low against the U.S. dollar before the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rebounded. Late Wednesday, the U.S. dollar was changing hands at 8,000.00 rupiah, down from its intraday high of 8,425.00 rupiah but up from 7,350.00 rupiah late Tuesday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  Thailand's baht, meanwhile, closed at an all-time low at 53.10 baht to the U.S. dollar in trading in Bangkok compared with 52.15 baht late Tuesday. At Wednesday's closing level, the baht is down 51% since the Thai government abandoned its fixed \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  system last July 2. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere in Asia, South Korea's won closed at 1,745 won to the U.S. dollar, down from 1,742 won late Tuesday in Asia, while the Philippine peso ended at a new low of 46.50 pesos to the dollar compared with 45.46 pesos Tuesday. But the Hong Kong dollar climbed to HK$7.7360 to the U.S. dollar, up sharply from HK$7.7500 late Tuesday, amid speculation Asia's central banks may pull together a massive joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  effort later in London and New York. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2  1136 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329du17009k3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
