\par\par \b \uc2  Asia base metal trade hammered by  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  blight. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Rene Pastor \par \uc2 474 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Januar 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Jan 6 (Reuters) - The Asian base metal trade was pounded on Tuesday by imploding regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with business in the region practically at a standstill. \par \par \uc2  "The Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been murder. There's not going to be any buying out of this area," a senior dealer for a Western metal trading house said. \par \par \uc2  Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tumbled like dominoes on Tuesday, with most touching fresh record lows as they were steamrolled by the rampant U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah crashed to a historic low of 7,400 to the dollar, the Thai baht slid to 52, the Malaysian ringgit plumbed fresh depths of 4.20 and the Philippine peso sank to a record 44.35. \par \par \uc2  Even the Singapore dollar dipped to 1.7420 from the previous 1.7020/30 despite rumours the de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. \par \par \uc2  "It's absolutely crazy. Base metal business, especially in Southeast Asia, is at a standstill," the aluminium dealer of a European trading company said. \par \par \uc2  "The problem now is finding somebody here to buy the stuff," a dealer added. \par \par \uc2  Aluminium purchases in Indonesia, estimated at around 100,000 tonnes last year, may slide below 50,000 tonnes in 1998 and may even go lower. \par \par \uc2  "It's even getting worse. Who knows when all this will end," one exasperated trader said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said it was difficult to quantify immediately the impact of renewed turmoil in the area's financial markets on base metal demand, but the outlook in the region is grim. \par \par \uc2  "The atmosphere here is doom and gloom. All you're hearing about are cancelled construction contracts. The Chinese are not buying. No one is picking up any forward material in copper," one said. \par \par \uc2  One European dealer said he had received reports that buyers in South Korea were shipping out large quantities of the red metal, putting copper prices under further pressure. \par \par \uc2  "They're shipping it out very aggressively. That is putting a lot of pressure on copper prices," he said. \par \par \uc2  In Asian trade, three-month copper was quoted by the trade at 0400 GMT at around US$1,698/703 a tonne, slightly below the last trade on the London Metal Exchange on Monday of $1,702. \par \par \uc2  "Many consumers here are struggling to survive," one trader said. "They cannot afford to import at the moment. If they have to pay for their purchases in U.S. dollars, it's going to kill them." \par \par \uc2  Base metal traders said many of their customers scampered for the sidelines to ride out the crisis. \par \par \uc2  "Business is very, very slow. People are still trying to learn to cope with the situation," one said. \par \par \uc2  -- Singapore Newsroom 65-8703305, fax 65-7768112 \par \par \uc2  -- Email: singapore.newsroom@reuters.com \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926du16015vw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
