\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - Asia  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  hit by seasonal dollar demand. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 639 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Dezember 1997\par 10:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Dec 30 (Reuters) - Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  yielded to commercial demand for dollars in otherwise uninspired trade on Tuesday as activity wound down for the end of the year. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won shed early gains and fell sharply on dollar demand from importers and for year-end interest payments on foreign debts. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said news that major U.S., European and Japanese lenders were planning to roll over loans to South Korean banks had been largely priced in by the market. \par \par \uc2  "All the good news is already factored in. And there's still a lot of corporate demand for dollars," said a Japanese bank dealer in \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Even \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and reports that banks in the U.S., Europe and Japan were considering extending new credit to Seoul of as much as $10 billion failed to lift the won in late trade. \par \par \uc2  "What we want to see and hear more than anything else is an immediate inflow of real foreign hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the market," a local bank dealer in Seoul said. "If not, all news will be just an empty announcement." \par \par \uc2  Seoul traders said the Bank of Korea sold dollars at 1,640 won in a clear attempt to cap the dollar's surge. \par \par \uc2  But the won closed weak at 1,590 to the dollar against its opening 1,220 and Monday's 1,395 close. \par \par \uc2  It had opened sharply higher as some local banks tried to push the dollar down in an attempt to reduce their foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses before book closing. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar ended higher at T$32.600 to the U.S. dollar against Monday's T$32.686 close, boosted by bargain-hunting interest in an otherwise slow market. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar ended near its day low on consistent year-end demand for the U.S. dollar while forwards remained firm in line with higher domestic interbank rates. \par \par \uc2  In Southeast Asia, the Indonesian rupiah stayed soft but was off its lows against the dollar amid fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Dollar/rupiah is the most well bid of all the dollar/regionals. But the market is on the defensive because of Bank Indonesia. There's talk they'll \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  today or tomorrow," the Japanese bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars for rupiah at the 5,300 level late on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said companies had placed dollar buying orders at various levels on expectations the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again to boost the rupiah for the year end. \par \par \uc2  They said short-dated swaps recovered above par as operators began boosting their rupiah reserves. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will be closed on Wednesday for book-closing and on Thursday for the New Year holiday. Trading will resume on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht was little changed at 47.15/40 to the dollar onshore at 0930 GMT against 46.85/47.05 late on Monday in listless trade. \par \par \uc2  The market appeared to shrug off a slew of economic data including an increase in Thailand's current account surplus to a preliminary 26.3 billion baht in October from a revised 2.9 billion baht surplus in September. \par \par \uc2  The trade surplus also widened to 23.3 billion baht in October from 7.9 billion baht in September, but Thailand's balance of payments fell into a 185 billion baht deficit in November from a 58.3 billion baht surplus in October. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit remained under pressure from commercial dollar demand, but dealers did not expect it to weaken beyond 3.93 to the dollar for the time being. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar slithered through the 1.67 level to the U.S. dollar in dull trade. \par \par \uc2  Philippine markets are closed for holidays until January 2. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtcu00kx1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
