\par\par \b \uc2  New round of Singapore rate hikes begins, more seen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Josephine Ng \par \uc2 544 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Dezember 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Dec 29 (Reuters) - A second round of prime rate hikes began in Singapore on Monday and analysts said they feared there could be further rises which would hurt the economy. \par \par \uc2  "My feeling is that there are quite high risks of another round" of prime rate hikes, said Eddie Lee, regional economist at Vickers Ballas. \par \par \uc2  Lee said Asia's economic crisis could drive Singapore banks to raise rates again in the next three to six months after OCBC Bank said on Monday it was raising its prime rate to seven percent from 6.5 percent with immediate effect. \par \par \uc2  OCBC lifted its prime rate by 50 basis points on December 2. Other banks followed suit and were expected to do so again in the next few days, the analysts said. \par \par \uc2  They said OCBC's new move was not surprising given the firmness in interbank rates, which surged to 10 percent earlier this month and are now around seven percent. \par \par \uc2  "Basically, you could see another 50-100 basis points as long as interbank rates stay at these levels," said Maminder Singh of Nomura Securities. \par \par \uc2  Further rate rises could hurt a Singapore economy already feeling the chills from the regional financial crisis which has swept through Southeast Asia and is now engulfing South Korea, the analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "The problem is most things are already slowing and the last thing you want is for interest rates to move up sharply," Singh said. \par \par \uc2  "A 100-point hike is already very tough on consumers, especially on those with housing loans. Persoanlly I wouldn't want to see higher prime rates from here," said Lee. \par \par \uc2  A key question was whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS), the de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if prime rates continued to rise, the analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "We did expect them to put more money into the system to sort of help the interbank market, but that would also weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar," Singh said. \par \par \uc2  The MAS was not expected to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at this stage, but there could be greater pressure to do so later on, Singh said. \par \par \uc2  The analysts said prime rates would remain stable at best in the near term. \par \par \uc2  "Overnight rates have come down very sharply. There is a chance that at least over the next month things would stabilise for a while, but you really want to watch what happens in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," said Lee. \par \par \uc2  Lim Chung Chun, head of research at ING Baring, said interbank rates could soften only in the second half of 1998. \par \par \uc2  He thought banks might not raise prime rates more than 100 basis points in the meantime. \par \par \uc2  Singh at Nomura said rates were unlikely to ease as the interbank market was expected to remain tight for the next three to six months. \par \par \uc2  He said banks were looking at firm interbank rates as a longer-term trend and so were unlikely to cut back prime rates. \par \par \uc2  Lee said even if interbank rates came off, banks would hold prime rates steady and take advantage of the wider margins. \par \par \uc2  "They might as well have some cushion," he said. \par \par \uc2  -- Singapore Newsroom (65) 870-3080; Fax (65) 776-8112 \par \par \uc2  -- Email: singapore.newsroom@reuters.com \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtct00qhs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
