\par\par \b \uc2  More blues in store for Singapore dollar-analysts. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 594 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Dezember 1997\par 03:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Dec 21 (Reuters) - Singapore's long resilient dollar will remain prey to the weakness plaguing Asia's frazzled \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but its fall should be far more dignified, analysts said at the weekend. \par \par \uc2  They said it was likely to resume its gradual descent to keep up with the much worse-hit \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of its neighbours as Asia's six-month-old crisis showed no signs of ending. \par \par \uc2  "The current corrective phase is technically inclined. The fundamental picture hasn't changed," said Thio Chin Loo, strategist at Banque Paribas in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "The Singapore dollar could easily hit 1.70 by year end and 1.80 by the first quarter," she added. \par \par \uc2  While once practically unimaginable, these levels are still a far cry from the more bearish forecasts of 2.00 and 2.10 that dealers were calling for when the Singapore dollar dived to six-year lows of 1.7175 early last week. \par \par \uc2  Shrinking liquidity in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and a perception Singapore's dollar had to fall further for Singapore to remain competitive with its neighbours induced a selling spree. \par \par \uc2  The normally static \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lost nearly seven percent in three trading days. \par \par \uc2  "We were hearing people saying the market was thin in other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  so the Sing dollar was the only thing they could sell, which is ridiculous," said an analyst who declined to be identified. \par \par \uc2  But apparent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS), the yen's recovery against the U.S. dollar and higher domestic interbank rates helped the Singapore dollar recover to around 1.66 in late Asian trade on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said more was almost certainly in store despite a rare statement from the MAS, the de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that it saw no reason for the Singapore dollar to weaken given strong domestic economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  Singapore officials have said previously they were not concerned about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide against the U.S. dollar, leaving the exchange rate to market forces while other Asian central banks scrambled to shore up their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the MAS comments highlighted Singapore's dilemma as a highly trade-dependent, exposed economy. \par \par \uc2  "On the one hand, there's the real threat of competition from the closer exporter countries like Malaysia, Taiwan. On the other hand, they would be concerned about import inflation," said Chia Woon Khien, head of Asian research at Skandinaviska Enskilda Banken. \par \par \uc2  "And the bigger concern is the stability of the financial markets. Just like Hong Kong, they would not want a Sing dollar that's going to be spinning downwards because it would affect a lot of investment interests in this country," she said. \par \par \uc2  But with genuine U.S. dollar demand from companies seeking to cover import requirements or for debt payments, the Singapore dollar was likely to remain under pressure. \par \par \uc2  "Companies are hedging their positions up to six months down the road," said Jimmy Koh, regional economist at I.D.E.A. \par \par \uc2  He said the Singapore dollar had actually strengthened on a trade-weighted basis and given the relatively muted threat of imported inflation, government emphasis would be on curbing upward pressure on domestic interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "We expect the authorities to start providing liquidity for the market because to keep a firm Sing dollar via high interest rates would kill the economy. The property market is already extremely weak," he said. \par \par \uc2  Interbank rates came off highs of 10 percent on Friday on talk the MAS had been injecting funds in the interbank system. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtcl00sqq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
