\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Yen rebound props up calmer Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 920 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Dezember 1997\par 11:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Dec 18 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  extended their gains on Thursday, lifted by the yen's resurgence against the U.S. dollar in very thin year-end markets. \par \par \uc2  Talk of fresh \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of Japan after its repeated dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday knocked the dollar off its fragile perch above 127 yen and weakened the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  across the board, dealers said. It recovered slightly to stand at 127.47/57 yen at 1008 GMT. \par \par \uc2  But Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were expected to make only limited headway in the face of lingering corporate demand for dollars to meet year-end payments. \par \par \uc2  In the Philippines, the peso perked up on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's new measures to boost dollar liquidity, but traders said the bank's failure to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets pulled the peso off its highs. \par \par \uc2  It ended at 39.69 to the dollar after rising to 39.00 in early trade against Wednesday's 39.77 close. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen selling dollars at 39.50 pesos and 39.40 pesos after buying an estimated $1.5 million in opening deals, traders said. \par \par \uc2  They generally welcomed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's efforts to counter a shortage of dollars by agreeing with the Bankers Association of the Philippines (BAP) to provide up to $150 million in the spot market daily. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will also enter into non-deliverable forward contracts with banks whose clients are unhedged. \par \par \uc2  BAP president Deogracias Vistan said Philippine banks sold nearly $50 million to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday to help lift the peso. \par \par \uc2  Volume in the peso-dollar market totalled $195 million. It had shrunk to less than $10 million in recent days due to the shortage of dollars. \par \par \uc2  Manila shares were also heartened by the peso's recovery. The main stock index ended 2.74 percent up at 1,846.09 points. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah burst through the 5,000 level against the dollar for the first time since Monday as Jakarta companies unloaded dollars, sparking offshore sales in very thin markets. \par \par \uc2  It was quoted at 4,800/900 to the dollar, a near 11 percent gain from its opening of 5,300/5,500. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said fears of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the central banks of Indonesia, Japan and \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  in support of the rupiah after the Bank of Japan's dollar/yen sales also lifted the unit. \par \par \uc2  Indonesian President Suharto's first public appearance, in apparent good health, since doctors advised him to rest put the rupiah on relatively solid footing early in the day. \par \par \uc2  Suharto, attending a graduation parade for military academy cadets, called for national resilience to overcome the country's economic and financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar strengthened towards 1.66 to the U.S. dollar against levels of 1.6735/75 late on Wednesday as discouraging noises from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore (MAS) and high domestic interbank rates curbed a selling frenzy. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they did not detect the MAS behind Thursday's U.S. dollar sales. \par \par \uc2  But they remained wary of testing the de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's resolve after apparent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday and Wednesday and a statement that it saw no reason for the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken given Singapore's sound fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  Vague rumours the government intended to liberalise the use of funds from Singapore's state savings scheme for the purchase of private property also lifted the Singapore dollar in late dealings, though dealers were largely dismissive of the talk. \par \par \uc2  "Basically they're looking for excuses to sell dollar/Sing and the market is exaggerated because the volume's very thin," a U.S. bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar's gains were expected to be capped by U.S. dollar demand near 1.65. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit whipped around in thin markets, firming to 3.7833/933 to the dollar from 3.8000/9000 five hours earlier. Dealers said its gains were blocked by corporate dollar demand near 3.77 ringgit, while the stock market's firm tone limited declines. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht bounced higher as exporters sold dollars following the baht's recovery in offshore markets where players unwound long dollar positions. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Thailand's downward revision of its 1997 current account deficit forecast and its projection of a rise in foreign reserves to $29 billion at the end of 1997 from $26.3 billion at the end of November also helped the baht. \par \par \uc2  It rose to 45.00/10 to the dollar onshore against 47.10/30 late on Wednesday. The offshore rate was at 43.75/44.25 against 45.80/46.30. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar ended sharply up at T$32.200 to the U.S. dollar from Wednesday's T$32.520 close as players booked profit on the subsiding U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Taipei traders said exporters also started selling the U.S. dollar, which hit a decade-high on Tuesday, after it failed to hold above the T$32.50 level in early trade. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar crept up from the 7.75 level against the U.S. dollar, edging to 7.7475/85 against 7.7490/7.7500 late on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen injecting funds continuously in very small quantities of around HK$100million-HK$200 million to ease local interbank rates. \par \par \uc2  South Korean financial markets were closed for the country's presidential election. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtci00yri\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
