\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore MAS Sees 'No Reason' For S$ To Weaken \b0\par\par\uc2 146 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Dezember 1997\par 11:09\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE (AP-Dow Jones)--The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore, the country's effective \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Wednesday there is no reason for the Singapore dollar to weaken. \par \par \uc2  'As Singapore's economic foundations remain sound, we see no reason why the Singapore dollar should weaken,' the authority said in a statement. \par \par \uc2  Tuesday, participants in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market said the MAS had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, which reached a six-year low, rising above S$1.70 against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  In Wednesday's statement, the MAS said, 'The MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  from time to time to iron out short-term fluctuations. We do not publicize our exchange rate \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .' \par \par \uc2  About 1000 GMT (5 a.m. EST), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.6714. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 17-12-97 \par \par \uc2  1009GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dtch09p1c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
