\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  take heart as yen surges. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 1,977 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Dezember 1997\par 06:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Dec 17 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  calmed down a bit on Wednesday as the dollar dropped dramatically against the yen following the Bank of Japan's unexpected -- and repeated -- \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and news of a massive Japanese income tax cut. \par \par \uc2  The dollar lost what had looked like a firm grip above the 131 yen level and fell as low as 125.85 yen in Tokyo trade. \par \par \uc2  But no one was prepared to predict the end of its reign over Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  just yet. \par \par \uc2  "Overall, sentiment in the whole of Asia is still very fragile so what we're seeing today may just be a technical rebound. I think the dollar demand is very real," said Jacqueline Ong, regional economist at I.D.E.A. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Southeast Asia, where the region's economic crisis had its beginning, had a much better day. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso reversed course, the Singapore dollar recovered from a heavy fall, the Malaysian ringgit moved higher and the Indonesian rupiah stopped falling. \par \par \uc2  The peso bounced to 39.904/937 to the U.S. dollar at 0530 GMT after hitting an all-time low of 41.60 earlier. \par \par \uc2  Manila traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen selling dollars heavily at various levels in an effort to counter dollar demand amid shrinking supply. \par \par \uc2  News the Bankers Association of the Philippines (BAP) and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had agreed to set up a dollar pool to cover the requirements of oil companies and other corporations helped boost the peso, they said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Gabriel Singson said the bank had also agreed to enter into non-deliverable forward contracts with dollar borrowers to ease demand in the spot market. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar also staged a recovery as talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in overnight trade and possibly early on Wednesday by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore (MAS), the de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , spooked the market. \par \par \uc2  It was quoted at 1.6885/915 to the U.S. dollar at 0530 GMT after firming through the 1.6800 level against a morning low of 1.7010. \par \par \uc2  "The MAS was in in London last night. When dollar/Sing got to a high of 1.7175, they were in selling," a senior regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader said. \par \par \uc2  "I heard they were in again through some local banks this morning. But then again, other local banks were bidding on the other side," he said. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar caved in to mounting pressure and dived through the key 1.70 level in thin London trade on Tuesday, reaching lows not seen in over six years. \par \par \uc2  The sharp drop meant the once static \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had lost nearly seven percent in three trading days as poor year-end liquidity exaggerated movements. \par \par \uc2  Dealers and analysts said it remained in a downtrend but the market was likely to avoid the 1.70 level for the time being. \par \par \uc2  "They (the MAS) don't mind a weaker Sing. In fact, they'd welcome it for now, but not too fast," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit also pulled back from its lows on the yen's rebound and on talk of foreign inflows to the stock market. It was at 3.8300/400 to the dollar against 3.8825/925 late on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the thin market was keeping the ringgit volatile, with small volumes causing wild gyrations. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah hovered between 5,450 and 5,600 to the dollar all morning as spreads narrowed to 50/100 points amid dwindling interest. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the dollar maintained a firm undertone against the rupiah, which gained little mileage from news that President Suharto would attend a public function on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Worries about his health have sent the rupiah spiralling to new lows in the past week. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht was little changed at 47.80/48.00 to the dollar onshore against 47.60/48.10 late on Tuesday. The offshore rate stood at 46.65/85 against 46.90/47.20. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they expected the baht to have a steadier day as regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  should gain from the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  In north Asia, the South Korean won slipped for the first time this week due to dollar buying for importer settlements due early next week. \par \par \uc2  The won was at 1,505/10 to the dollar against Tuesday's 1,425 close, but dealers said its decline would be limited by fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar continued to be driven by external factors, firming to T$32.644/669 against Tuesday's T$32.820 close as traders watched the yen rebound. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar was protected from weakening through the 7.75 level to the U.S. dollar by thin year-end conditions and the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not appear to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, but it was seen offering three-month money in early trade. \par \par \uc2  A European bank dealer in Singapore said the ringgit's ability to hold above the 3.93 per dollar level for the second time on Tuesday suggested i \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso and the Singapore dollar had the most volatile morning, both rebounding sharply against the U.S. dollar \par \par \uc2  Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  posted fresh losses on Tuesday as persistent dollar demand and dwindling trading volumes sparked fresh carnage in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  Only the South Korean won defied logic, surging nearly 16 percent to highs of 1,350 against the dollar after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decided late on Monday to scrap its 10 percent trading band. It ended at 1,425.0 against Monday's 1,563.9 close. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the won was benefitting from expectations of heavy dollar inflows under the IMF's $57 billion bailout scheme for South Korea, which had moved players to unwind long dollar positions built during the won's near 50 percent drop last week. \par \par \uc2  The government's decision to float the won, as requested by the International Monetary Fund (IMF), sell off one or two troubled banks and push forward with a $10 billion sovereign bond issue boosted Korean financial markets on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said sentiment could quickly turn negative if more of the pledged IMF loans were not disbursed soon. \par \par \uc2  The won was also expected to face selling pressure from importers seeking dollars towards the weekend. \par \par \uc2  Its improved fortunes gave the Taiwan dollar an opening boost, but it soon shed the gains in the face of strong U.S. dollar demand from importers. \par \par \uc2  Taipei traders said there was also concern about the previously sturdy Singapore dollar's sharp falls, which triggered renewed fears of a competitive depreciation in the Taiwan dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar ended at T$32.820 to the U.S. dollar, its lowest close since November 1987, against Monday's T$32.746 close. It hit a low of T$32.950. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the last minute of trade in a bid to keep its dollar above the psychological T$33 level. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar remained on the cusp of the key 7.75 level to the U.S. dollar, but forwards eased in the wake of the won's rebound. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  was heard buying the local dollar via a major commercial bank to limit the U.S. dollar's rise. \par \par \uc2  Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were off their lows but remained weak and weary as companies scrambled to buy dollars to meet loan payments amid shrinking liquidity and widening spreads. \par \par \uc2  "There's just a lack of liquidity in the market. There are very few interbank players around so if you're buying, the whole world knows you're buying," said Jimmy Koh, regional economist at I.D.E.A. in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "Given the widening spreads, the pace of depreciation is much faster. People are just extremely bearish on the region," he added. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said transaction volumes had fallen sharply as players sought to limit their exposure to growing market volatility towards the year end. \par \par \uc2  "In the middle of the year, people would trade something like 20 to 50 (million) dollars. But lately because of the volatility and the fact that the big figure changes so fast ... positions have been reduced to one to two (million) dollars," said a European bank dealer in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah recovered in late trade as attempts to test the 6,000 level failed, causing a pullback in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  which was reinforced by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  It was at 5,500/600 to the dollar at 1020 GMT after Bank Indonesia sold dollars at 5,700, dealers said. The rupiah hit an early record low of 5,980 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said it remained fragile on lingering worries about President Suharto's health despite clear signs he was gradually resuming work after a period of rest and corporate difficulties in meeting debt payments. \par \par \uc2  They said liquidity had practically dried up and spreads of 200 or 300 points were exacerbating the rupiah's moves. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht's abrupt recovery from a record low of 49.35 against the dollar sparked speculation of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  support. \par \par \uc2  Bangkok dealers said exporters sold dollars around the 48 baht level, but the Thai unit remained weak at 47.50/48.00 to the dollar against 47.30/90 late on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Offshore baht was at 46.90/47.20 against 46.90/47.40. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Tarrin Nimmanahaeminda appealed to Thais to refrain from speculating against the baht and said the government would use the limited resources at hand to defend it. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso ended at a record low of 40.214 to the dollar against a previous 38.853 close despite \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Further falls were only prevented by the activation of volatility bands. \par \par \uc2  Poor market liquidity -- total turnover was a mere $9.5 million -- did little for the peso's condition and dealers expected more declines. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Gabriel Singson said the peso would only recover if people stopped speculating against it. \par \par \uc2  Dealers attributed the peso's steep drop to panic buying of dollars by firms with dollar exposures and importers hedging future dollar requirements. \par \par \uc2  A slowdown in remittances from overseas contract workers was also contributing to the shortage of dollars. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar fell within a hair's breadth of the key 1.70 level to the U.S. dollar, but fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  pulled it back from the edge, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  It was at its weakest in more than six years at 1.6935/55 to the U.S. dollar at 1020 GMT after hitting a low of 1.6995. Late on Monday it was at 1.6835/65. \par \par \uc2  There was no consensus among dealers about the likelihood of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore (MAS) selling U.S. dollars above the 1.70 level, or over whether it was behind a spurt of dollar sales that pushed the Singapore dollar up to an early high of 1.6660. \par \par \uc2  "I think they were in at least once or twice this morning, but it didn't really help because dollar/Sing is still biddish," a U.S. bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  I.D.E.A.'s Koh was skeptical about the MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  given the sharp falls in other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The authorities have affirmed again and again that they look at the Singapore dollar, not just against the U.S. dollar, but on a trade-weighted basis," he said. \par \par \uc2  The MAS declined to confirm rumours about an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit recovered as players took profit on the dollar's gains after the rupiah rose on Bank Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  It firmed to 3.8825/925 to the dollar against 3.9025/125 late on Monday. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtch00w7l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
