\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Asian stocks battered by Korea, but HK rises. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chris Pizzey \par \uc2 682 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Dezember 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Dec 12 (Reuters) - Asian stock markets continued to be battered by fallout from the financial crisis in South Korea, but contrarians enjoyed pointing out rises in Hong Kong and Taiwan. \par \par \uc2  Korea's economic woes tended to dominate sentiment throughout Asia. But some markets had doemstic travails of their own, and this included Jakarta, where news that President Suharto had cancelled a trip to Malaysia next week prompted a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock market rout. \par \par \uc2  A poor day in the region was guaranteed after the Dow Jones industrial average fell 129.80 points, or 1.6 percent, to 7,848.99 overnight. \par \par \uc2  Investors there were perturbed by further warnings from U.S. companies that Asia's problems were having a direct impact on their bottom lines. \par \par \uc2  The last thing sentiment needed was another corporate collapse in Korea -- but it got one. \par \par \uc2  Dongsuh Securities Co Ltd, one of Korea's leading brokerage houses, filed for court receivership -- the latest in a series of corporate collapses. \par \par \uc2  As if this wasn't enough, the Korean won initially performed its now habitual routine of plunging its 10 percent limit down on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  In fact, the won later benefited from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and ended the day slightly firmer at 1,710 to the dollar against Thursday's close of 1,719.8 and the limit low of 1,891.4. \par \par \uc2  But the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at a European bank in \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  doubted the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would work in the future. "The Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't got enough dollars to stop the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hitting 2,000 in a very short time," he said. \par \par \uc2  The Seoul stock market duly took its cue from the gloomy scenario. It opened more than six percent down, failed to recover, and ended more than seven percent down. \par \par \uc2  The composite stock index closed at its lowest level in a decade at 350.68, a fall of 7.07 percent. \par \par \uc2  Park Young-chul, chief trader at Hyundai Securities, called the collapsing market a "tragedy". \par \par \uc2  "The market is cut loose from the normal framework. There are no rules applying...right now," he said. \par \par \uc2  The dubious honour of the regions biggest percentage decline belonged to Jakarta. \par \par \uc2  Stocks there -- which had already been affected by Korea's malaise -- were sent into a tailspin by an announcement that Presiden Suharto would not after all be attending next week's Association of South East Asian Nations (ASEAN) summit in Kuala Lumpur. \par \par \uc2  Earlier this week, stocks and the Indonesian rupiah had been sold heavily on rumours that the 76-year-old leader was seriously ill, or even dead. \par \par \uc2  But it was then announced he would attend the meeting and prices perked up. \par \par \uc2  Today's news of his non-attendance came as a body-blow. \par \par \uc2  At one stage, the Jakarta stock index was off more than nine percent. It ended at 365.85, down 7.64 percent. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah also plunged, crashing through the 5,000 level. In late trading it was quoted at a staggering 5,150.00 to the U.S. dollar compared with yesterday's finish of 4,505.00. \par \par \uc2  One \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a bank in Singapore said, "This news is disastrous for Indonesia. They told us he (Suharto) was well and now they say he's not going to KL (Kuala Lumpur)." \par \par \uc2  "Can you believe one guy was quoted as saying Suharto can't travel long distances. Well, he's just come back from South Africa. How far is KL from Jakarta, two hours (flight)? He's ill. Sell," he said. \par \par \uc2  Markets in Tokyo, Manila, Singapore, Bangkok and Kuala Lumpur all fell. \par \par \uc2  But Hong Kong and Taiwan bucked the trend, manging to eke out gains. \par \par \uc2  In Hong Kong, the Hang Seng index closed at the day's high of 10,614.66, a gain of 1.87 percent. Dealers said this reflected a technical rebound after three days of losses. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan dealers went bargain-hunting and the weighted index ended 1.54 percent higher at 8,393.27. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtcc017ag\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
