\par\par \b \uc2  Indonesia In Talks With Singapore On Aid For Companies \b0\par\par\uc2 222 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Dezember 1997\par 04:22\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR -(Dow Jones)- Indonesia is holding talks on redirecting money pledged by Singapore to defend the rupiah into assisting Indonesian companies suffering from high interest rates and a liquidity squeeze, a senior official of the Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed. \par \par \uc2  But the official said the amount of money to be used hasn't been agreed upon and "still under negotiation." \par \par \uc2  "The exact amount of money we hope to use is still uncertain, and our Finance Ministry is discussing the issue with Singapore," the official said. "It's still uncertain." \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets several times to defend the rupiah since Indonesia reached agreement with the International Monetary Fund in October. Separately, Singapore has pledged $5 billion toward the IMF's rescue package for Indonesia. \par \par \uc2  Singapore officials have said the amount of money to be spent on supporting the rupiah is undetermined. They have also made clear that the $5 billion pledge is contingent on Indonesia fulfilling the terms of its IMF program. \par \par \uc2  Indonesian Minister of Industry and Trade Tunky Ariwibowo last week that the government was seeking to use Singapore money to help small-scale and export-oriented companies with liquidity in the current environement. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1997 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020011006dtc1014nk\par }\p