\par\par \b \uc2  FEATURE - Cash plus confidence make Singapore safe haven. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chris Johnson \par \uc2 1,077 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 November 1997\par 04:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Nov 30 (Reuters) - Don't go to Singapore if you're looking for second-hand Rolex watches at knock-down prices or Mercedes convertibles from bankrupt investors. \par \par \uc2  As the 'For Sale' signs go up on the condominiums of Bangkok and Hong Kong, Singapore has remained an island of relative calm in the financial storm raging across Asia. \par \par \uc2  True, the Singapore dollar has depreciated by about 13 percent against the U.S. dollar and the stock market has lost about a quarter of its value this year. \par \par \uc2  The great property boom of the early 1990s is also over and modest price falls have stopped speculators making a fortune buying apartments from the blueprints and reselling them before completion. \par \par \uc2  But the economy has remained almost unscathed by the turmoil that has rocked banks from Tokyo to Jakarta this year. \par \par \uc2  And it is likely to stay that way. Even pessimistic forecasters say Singapore economic growth may slip only a point or two next year from the 7-10 percent range of recent times. \par \par \uc2  Economists argue the keys to Singapore's remarkable stability are cash and confidence: it has no foreign debt and its tightly regulated financial sector inspires trust. \par \par \uc2  "Singapore just doesn't have the potential for the kind of balance of payments crisis seen elsewhere in the region," said Desmond Supple, head of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Barclays Capital Group. \par \par \uc2  "And the regulatory environment is very firm. In the past the Singapore authorities have been criticised for being overly cautious in the regulation of the financial sector. Their approach has been fully vindicated by what has happened now. The banking sector is stable," Supple said. \par \par \uc2  Exploiting its location at the crossroads of Asia, Singapore has nurtured a busy entrepot trade since independence in 1965. \par \par \uc2  Next to the world's most active shipping route between the South China Sea and the Indian Ocean, Singapore has one of the world's busiest ports and has used this to develop a financial sector now among the top four \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  Bequeathed a solid infrastructure by its former British colonists, the ruling People's Action Party (PAP) of patriarch Lee Kuan Yew has built up huge \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves which topped Singapore $118 billion (US$74 billion) in September. \par \par \uc2  And through a compulsory government-controlled savings scheme, it has encouraged a high level of domestic savings, which have then been used to avoid borrowing abroad. \par \par \uc2  Singapore is now the world's sixth largest capital exporter and has an annual current account surplus of US$16 billion. \par \par \uc2  Singapore's critics, and there are many, say the PAP, which won 81 of the 83 parliamentary seats at the last election in January 1997, runs the country like a corporation. \par \par \uc2  They say it only pays lip service to democracy, stifles the press and regards citizenship as something that can be bestowed or removed like a company which can hire or fire its workers. \par \par \uc2  PAP stands for "Pay And Pay", they say, citing the numerous government levies and indirect taxes such as charges of up to S$70,000 (US$44,000) for the right to buy a car. \par \par \uc2  But Lee, prime minister from 1959 to 1990 and still in cabinet as senior minister, shrugs off critics who call the Singapore style of government authoritarian or autocratic, a label he rejects. \par \par \uc2  "If I ran a Western-style democracy and took a straw poll and went according to the poll, we would have come to grief," he told Reuters in an interview this month, describing his experiences as prime minister. \par \par \uc2  And with direct and corporate taxation rates low and excellent communication links to the rest of the region, Singapore is the first choice for many multinational companies. \par \par \uc2  Business leaders say the government's conduct during the continuing economic turmoil has been admirable. \par \par \uc2  By contrast with Malaysian Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad, whose trenchant attacks on speculators in the financial markets have sent funds scurrying to sell the Malaysian ringgit, Singapore Prime Minister Goh Chok Tong has soothed markets. \par \par \uc2  "I take the view that you can't fight the market," Goh told reporters in Tokyo in August. \par \par \uc2  Although the de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , does \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets from time to time, it appears to be content to let the Singapore dollar find its own level. \par \par \uc2  Economists say that level is probably within a few percent of where it is at the moment at 1.59 to the U.S. dollar -- not much changed from around 1.40 at the beginning of the year. \par \par \uc2  "Both from a macro-economic point of view and from a corporate point of view, the country is doing quite well," said Bruce Gale, analyst at Political & Economic Risk Consultancy. \par \par \uc2  "It has very large foreign reserves to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  if it wanted to and no one can pull out their funds because there is no debt." \par \par \uc2  Singapore has also been partly insulated from the current regional crisis by its mix of industries. \par \par \uc2  On top of its finance and business sectors, which account for about 28 percent of gross domestic product (GDP), Singapore's commerce, entrepot, transport and communications industries provide another 31-32 percent of output. \par \par \uc2  Only 26 percent of GDP is manufacturing and more than half of this is in electronics or related industries, which sell most of their wares to developed economies. Sales from this sector to the region tend to be of semi-finished goods. \par \par \uc2  The global electronics industry slumped in 1996, trimming GDP growth back a little, and it may slow again in 1998 or 1999, but there has been modest growth this year in the sector. \par \par \uc2  Singapore is reluctant to say so, but most economists argue it could teach the rest of the region a few tricks. \par \par \uc2  "What has characterised all the countries that have been battered in the crisis, is that they are massive capital importers," said Supple. "If you are a capital importer, you are vulnerable to a flight of capital." \par \par \uc2  "A country cannot grow just by borrowing from abroad. Financing excessive growth leads to escalating foreign debt and widening current account deficits. Singapore is an example of how that should be avoided," he said. \par \par \uc2  Manmindar Singh, senior economist at Nomura Securities in Singapore agreed: "If there is a lesson it's this: 'You should only finance what you can afford.'" \par \par \uc2  ($1 = S$1.59) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtbu01xzt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
