\par\par \b \uc2  Bk Indonesia/MAS/BOJ -2: Soedradjad Fails To Elaborate \b0\par\par\uc2 148 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 November 1997\par 04:50\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA (AP-Dow Jones)--Bank Indonesia Governor Soedradjad Djiwandono stressed Monday that joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  between the central banks of Indonesia, \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Japan was still a viable option to keep the rupiah trading at respectable levels. \par \par \uc2  Speaking before Indonesia's House of Representatives Monday, Soedradjad said Bank Indonesia, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore, and the Bank of Japan will act 'when needed,' but failed to elaborate. \par \par \uc2  He noted how joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on November 3 was able to drive the rupiah roughly 9% higher against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Since that time, however, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has fallen back into a weakening trend and Singapore and Jakarta dealers feel that the 3,700 level versus the dollar is likely later in the week. \par \par \uc2  Currently, the rupiah is trading at 3,630 versus the dollar. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 24-11-97 \par \par \uc2  0350GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dtbo08zs5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
