\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Volatile dollar overshadows Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 901 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 1997\par 06:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Nov 13 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  took a backseat on Thursday as the U.S. dollar's wild moves against the yen and the spectre of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of Japan (BOJ) stole centrestage. \par \par \uc2  The dollar slid from its New York close at 126.45/55 yen on talk the BOJ had sold dollars in Singapore markets. \par \par \uc2  But no confirmation was available and some dealers attributed the move to profit-taking and stop-loss dollar sales. \par \par \uc2  Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were weighed down by the dollar's strength but dealers said the market failed to shrug off the lethargic state it has been in this week. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah was lifted by talk Bank Indonesia had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Jakarta and \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to sell dollars from the 3,400 to 3,355 rupiah levels. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah was quoted at 3,375/80 to the dollar at 0515 GMT against its early low of 3,415 and 3,365/75 late on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Jakarta dealers described the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as "moderate" and said it seemed to be aimed at keeping the rupiah from breaching the 3,400 level. \par \par \uc2  "It's something that they certainly have to do because letting the rupiah fall below that psychological barrier would lead to more uneasiness, particularly among local corporates," a foreign bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  There was no clear sign of coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the BOJ or the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS) as seen last week, Singapore dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Japan's Finance Ministry declined to comment on whether the BOJ had taken part in the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The MAS was not immediately available for comment. \par \par \uc2  "There seems to be good support for the dollar at 3,360 rupiah so it should trade in a 3,350-3,410 range," a U.S. bank dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Thai baht slipped further to 38.70/80 to the dollar onshore against 38.55/65 late on Wednesday. The offshore rate was at 38.38/48 against 37.999/00. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said trade was subdued as players awaited the announcement of Thai Prime Minister Chuan Leekpai's new cabinet. Chuan said he had completed the formation of his cabinet and would send the list to King Bhumibol Adulyadej in the evening. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was little reaction to comments by International Monetary Fund (IMF) Managing Director Michel Camdessus that the Fund had no plans to make changes to Thailand's economic programme. \par \par \uc2  Camdessus told a news conference in Singapore, where he made a one-day stop as part of a Southeast Asia tour, that Thailand had passed its first review by the IMF. \par \par \uc2  In Bangkok, Supachai Panitchpakdi, expected to be named deputy premier in charge of economic affairs in the new cabinet, appeared to echo the IMF chief's statements. \par \par \uc2  He said Thailand did not intend to renegotiate the terms of the IMF-led $17.2 billion economic bailout package. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar eased to 1.5803/23 to the U.S. dollar against 1.5746/66 on Wednesday on buying of the Malaysian ringgit/Singapore dollar cross. \par \par \uc2  "There's been some ringgit/Sing buying going through. There's talk that the cross might go to 50 cents but I think it has to break 48.20 first," a European bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The ringgit was at 47.62/79 Singapore cents at 0515 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Across the border, the ringgit showed little sign of stirring out of the range it has established against the U.S. dollar in recent sessions. It was steady at 3.3100/00. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso slipped to 33.58/68 per dollar against a previous close of 33.43 but further falls were expected to be limited on fund inflows to equity and fixed income instruments and from overseas contract workers. \par \par \uc2  In north Asia, the Korean won held up for the third straight day as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars at 988.00 won in opening trade while repeating its daily words of support. \par \par \uc2  "Our stance to maintain the won steadily stronger has not changed," said Lee Eung-baek, chief of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  division. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Korea has moved its defence line up by one won to the dollar for each of the last two days, steering the embattled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  away from the 1,000 level eyed by the market. \par \par \uc2  Seoul traders said the market was also on hold for a government package of measures to stabilise financial markets. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar slid to T$31.214/245 against its previous T$30.992 close on jitters about the weak yen and won. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar was steady but easier at 7.7345/55 to the U.S. dollar from Wednesday's 7.7300/10 close. \par \par \uc2  Interbank rates and forwards also backed off as liquidity eased after a clarification by the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) regarding penal rates levied at its discount window. \par \par \uc2  On October 23, the HKMA had said it would levy penal rates on "repeated borrowers" at its discount window, making banks wary of tapping the facility and leading to a liquidity crunch. \par \par \uc2  The HKMA spelled out on Thursday that repeated borrowers are licensed banks which have borrowed through the facility on eight occasions in any period of 25 days or on four consecutive days in which the window is open. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtbd02llv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
