\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar rebounds after falling on BOJ rumours. \b0\par\par\uc2 375 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Nov 13 (Reuters) - ASIAN \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ACTIVITY: \par \par \uc2  0052 GMT --*Dollar rebounded above 126 yen after falling briefly as low as 125.78 yen partly on rumours that Bank of Japan (BOJ) might have sold dollars for yen in Singapore market, dealers said. *Dealers said, however, little sign of actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was detected. \par \par \uc2  *One dealer said Japanese newspaper report that Finance Ministry and Bank of Japan waiting for good time to sell dollars for yen may behind rumour of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Profit-taking sales were seen heavy at 126.50 yen. \par \par \uc2  *Mainichi Shimbun said on Thursday that MOF and BOJ have been considering dollar sales \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since last week. But they have been unable to find right time to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in market due to falling stocks and poor economic conditions, it said. \par \par \uc2  *Finance Minister Hiroshi Mitsuzuka said on Thursday that Hong Kong's G7 agreement on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  still intact, added that Japan will deal with \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues in appropriate and timely manner. \par \par \uc2  *Bids solid at around 125.80 yen from operators, including importers, who failed to pick up sufficient dollars in recent rally, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  0005 GMT --*Dollar eased against yen on profit-taking by interbank and overseas players, but greenback's downside seen limited due to bearish outlook for Japanese stocks. \par \par \uc2  *Profit-taking in mark against yen also indirectly weighed on dollar/yen earlier. \par \par \uc2  *Impact from Japan's September current account data was limited although surplus came in at upper-end of expected range, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  *Japan's current account surplus rose 63.7 percent in September from year earlier to 1.165 trillion yen, \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said. \par \par \uc2  *"The dollar slipped in early trade as some players locked in profits after its recent surge. But it is expected to meet strong bargain-hunting interest as Nikkei is highly likely to see another sharp fall today after overnight plunge in CME Nikkei futures," a dealer at a city bank. \par \par \uc2  *But Eisuke Sakakibara, vice finance minister for international affairs, again on Thursday declined to comment on exchange rates. \par \par \uc2  *He also declined to comment on rise in Japan's current account surplus. Tokyo Treasury Desk 81-3-3432-1396 \par \par \uc2  email:tokyo.newsroom@reuters,com \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtbd02lev\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
