\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  tread water as won recovers. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 943 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 November 1997\par 11:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Nov 11 (Reuters) - Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained in well-worn territory on Tuesday as the stricken Korean won managed to stave off further attacks with the help of its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the Bank of Korea stepped in to rescue the won from the brink of the psychological 1,000-to-the-dollar level in opening trade and later lowered its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  level. \par \par \uc2  The won ended at 989.90 to the dollar after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars aggressively at 999.90 won and then at 989.00 won. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Korea backed its action with strong verbal support, discouraging heavy won sales for the time being. \par \par \uc2  A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said the bank would strenuously support the won's "Maginot Line" of 1,000. \par \par \uc2  "We will absolutely defend the 1,000 won level to the dollar," a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said. "To this end, we will implement stronger measures." \par \par \uc2  Singapore dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's efforts had caused won non-deliverable forwards to come off their highs as well. \par \par \uc2  "The reason the forwards are moving to the left is because people are probably saying that the spot Korean won is not going to pass 1,000 anytime soon. They think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not going to let it," said one dealer. \par \par \uc2  But not everyone was convinced. \par \par \uc2  "Any strengthening in the won would be a correction. I don't foresee any help for the won in the short term," a senior regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  Korea's overnight call loan rate shot to a yearly high of 14.50 percent as merchant banks, saddled with bad debts following major bankruptcies, sought hard cash to buy dollars. \par \par \uc2  The government said it would soon announce a financial stabilisation package, including relief for the troubled banks. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere in north Asia, the Taiwan dollar ended weak but off its lows on the won's recovery and speculation the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the Taiwan dollar at the T$31 level to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  It ended at T$30.992 against Monday's T$30.985. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the Taiwan dollar's direction would continue to be driven by the won's moves due to intense competition between the two territories in key export markets. \par \par \uc2  "The only reason for the Taiwan dollar to cross 31.00 is if the won goes past 1,000," a Singapore dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar eased to 7.7315/45 to the U.S. dollar from 7.7295/10 at Monday's close, but dealers said there was little impact on the interbank market from Monday's run on International Bank of Asia. \par \par \uc2  However, some dealers said they expected borrowing costs of smaller banks to rise after the incident. Financial Secretary Donald Tsang said Hong Kong authorities would help International Bank of Asia meet its obligations. \par \par \uc2  In Southeast Asia, the Thai baht slid to 37.90/38.10 to the dollar onshore from 37.10/37.30 late on Monday as initial euphoria over the appointment of new Prime Minister Chuan Leekpai faded. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market was awaiting details of Chuan's new cabinet and concrete steps to rehabilitate the financial sector. \par \par \uc2  Chuan was quoted in an interview with a Thai newspaper as saying he expected to be able to revitalise confidence in the immediate term to halt capital flight from Thailand and solve liquidity problems. \par \par \uc2  "People are just waiting to see what the new man is going to do and how the government is going to shape up," said one dealer. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the baht's direction would also hinge on the ability of the government and private sector to persuade foreign creditors to roll over $21-$30 billion of short-term debt due by the end of this year. \par \par \uc2  The offshore baht rate eased to 37.84/94 to the dollar from Monday's 37.20/35 as unwinding of long dollar forward positions abated. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said the baht could head higher as the remainder of the positions, established when the baht came under speculative attack six months ago, matured by the end of the week. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso rose to close at 33.82 to the dollar from a previous 34.27, after breaching its first volatility band at 34.74 and hitting a high of 34.48. \par \par \uc2  Manila traders said the peso was boosted by large inflows from overseas contract workers repatriating their wages and talk a Hong Kong firm might have moved some funds to Philippine-based banks to buy shares in a local beer and food conglomerate. \par \par \uc2  After the market close, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Gabriel Singson said the peso's depreciation had been "over-exaggerated" and it was likely to correct in line with economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah hovered between 3,310 and 3,330 to the dollar, trapped between corporate dollar demand and fears of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the government's pledge on Monday to close 16 unhealthy banks despite potential political fallout had boosted support for the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  Joint \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the central banks of Japan, \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Indonesia to buy the rupiah last week had also helped stabilise the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the time being. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit edged up to 3.2960/60 to the dollar from Monday's 3.3150/250, but activity was confined to a very narrow range, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  A Standard & Poor's report saying it did not see Malaysia facing the same problems as Indonesia and Thailand helped prop up the ringgit, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtbb02q0q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
