\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Asia  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  mixed on Thai hopes,won woes. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 829 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 November 1997\par 06:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Nov 7 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mixed on Friday as traders veered between growing optimism of an opposition victory in the fight for Thailand's leadership and fears the South Korean economy was nearing crisis point. \par \par \uc2  Most Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were relatively steady, but North Asian markets showed signs of strain due to Seoul's difficulties and signs the Hong Kong dollar might face another speculative assault. \par \par \uc2  In Thailand, the baht bounced to 37.85/38.05 to the dollar at 0510 GMT against 38.90/39.10 late on Thursday as players unwound long dollar positions on expectations the baht would surge if the opposition Democrats succeeded in wooing smaller parties necessary to forming a parliamentary majority. \par \par \uc2  Thai stocks also jumped, with the composite SET index rising 4.64 percent to 500.52 points. \par \par \uc2  The baht was also up offshore at 38.42/52 to the dollar against 39.30/39.60 on Thursday, but Singapore dealers said investment houses were keen to pick up dollars on the way down. \par \par \uc2  "They still haven't decided who will take over. I'd think Chuan (Leekpai) would be good for the country but he's not a friend of Chavalit (Yongchaiyudh), so that could be difficult," a senior regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  Democrat party leader Chuan Leekpai and Chatichai Choonhavan, head of the Chart Pattana Party, are in a fierce contest to succeed Chavalit as prime minister. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah slipped to 3,300/10 to the dollar in slow trade as players remained wary of further coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  A legal challange by President Suharto's second son Bambang Trihatmodjo, who has filed a law suit following the government's closure of Bank Andromeda, which he part owns, also kept players cautious. \par \par \uc2  "The market is slow as players are staying sidelined, closely watching the government's stance over Bambang's legal challange," another dealer with a European bank said. \par \par \uc2  State Secretary Murdiono, however, said the government stood by the decision to close 16 privately-owned banks and said it was still legally effective. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said sentiment for the rupiah remained weak but fears the central banks of Japan and \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  might add their weight to any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Bank Indonesia would limit its declines to 3,340 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore (MAS) confirmed that it had bought rupiah with the Bank of Japan and Bank Indonesia twice this week. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit was at 3.2950/50 to the dollar, but talk of dollar offers by local banks around the 3.3300 level would cap its declines, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was at 1.5710/20 to the U.S. dollar, with dollar offers seen at 1.5770. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso traded sluggishly at 34.74/84 to the dollar against Thursday's 34.83 close. Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar offers at 34.95 pesos were shielding the peso. \par \par \uc2  In North Asia, the Korean won came under fire again, falling to new lows despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar sales at the 975 won and 980 won levels in early trade. \par \par \uc2  Talk that Korea would be forced to turn to the International Monetary Fund (IMF) for assistance as it dealt with a string of corporate defaults and shrinking foreign reserves continued to swirl around the market despite government denials. \par \par \uc2  IMF Managing Director Michel Camdessus backed Seoul's efforts to reassure investors. \par \par \uc2  "South Korea has taken, during recent months, courageous measures to prevent dangers. These measures...have produced the desired effect and in particular have contributed to a significant reduction in external imbalances," Camdessus said. \par \par \uc2  But dealers and analysts remained gloomy about the won. "Everybody is talking about the won reaching 1,000 to the dollar. They're saying the Bank of Korea is almost bankrupt after all these \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said one. \par \par \uc2  A senior Bank of Korea official denied newspaper reports that its foreign reserves may already be too depleted for a continued defendce of the won. \par \par \uc2  "The reports were much exaggerated and based on market rumours, some of them ill-intended," the official told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  Official figures put foreign reserves at $30.5 billion at end October, about 2.5 times Korea's monthly imports. \par \par \uc2  The won's woes depressed the Taiwan dollar as players worried that further depreciation in South Korea -- a key industrial rival -- would undermine Taiwan's export competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar was at T$30.888/927 against Thursday's T$30.700 close. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar edged up to 7.7295/15 to the U.S. dollar from Thursday's 7.7315/25 close. \par \par \uc2  Forwards and interbank rates firmed on fears of a speculative attack even as Camdessus praised Hong Kong's recent defence of its dollar and said the IMF "strongly endorses" the government's commitment to the peg to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtb702tw9\par }\page {\par\fs20