\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  eye Thai politics, weak won. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 1,032 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 November 1997\par 12:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Nov 7 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mixed on Friday as traders veered between growing optimism of an opposition victory in the fight for Thailand's leadership and fears the South Korean economy was nearing crisis point. \par \par \uc2  Most Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were relatively steady, but North Asian markets showed signs of strain due to Seoul's difficulties and signs the Hong Kong dollar might face another speculative assault. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht, on firm ground for much of the day, was buoyed further after opposition Democratic Party leader Chuan Leekpai said he had the support of enough members of parliament to form a new government. \par \par \uc2  The news came as a relief to Thai markets after days of wrangling between the Democrats and the Chart Pattana Party, whose leader Chatichai Choonhavan was also a front runner in the race to replace outgoing Prime Minister Chavalit Yongchaiyudh. \par \par \uc2  "Before the news, dollar/baht was trading around 39.00. It just dropped 50 pips on the news," a U.S. bank dealer in Singapore said. "But I don't know how long this enthusiasm can last," he added. \par \par \uc2  He said the baht was facing resistance from dollar demand offshore at 38.00 baht and at 37.50 baht onshore. \par \par \uc2  It stood at 37.90/38.10 to the dollar onshore at 0930 GMT against 38.90/39.10 late on Thursday. The offshore rate was at 38.00/50 against 39.30/60 previously. \par \par \uc2  Thai stocks also gained on the news, with the composite SET index up 3.08 percent to 493.04 at 0931 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Chuan, a former prime minister, was expected to be more effective in implementing economic reforms pegged to the International Monetary Fund's (IMF) $17.2 billion loan package. \par \par \uc2  "We are aware of the immediate needs of the public, that they want up to help shore up the economy," Chuan said. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah traded sluggishly as players kept a wary eye on simmering political tensions from the government's efforts to overhaul the financial sector by closing down 16 unhealthy banks. \par \par \uc2  An outburst by one of President Suharto's sons and court challenges by two banks linked to members of his family raised questions about the government's commitment to pushing through banking reforms. \par \par \uc2  However, State Secretary Murdiono said after meeting Suharto the government stood by its decision to close the privately-owned banks and that it was still legally effective. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said fears of further coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  also kept the rupiah petrified around the 3,290/00 level to the dollar, little changed from 3,280/90 on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  They said sentiment for the rupiah remained weak but fears the central banks of Japan and \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  might add their weight to any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Bank Indonesia would limit its declines to 3,340 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore confirmed it had bought rupiah with the Bank of Japan and Bank Indonesia twice this week. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit was at 3.2950/50 to the dollar, but talk of dollar offers by local banks around the 3.3300 level would cap its declines, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was at 1.5665/85 to the U.S. dollar, with dollar offers seen at 1.5770. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso ended steady at 34.85 to the dollar after Finance Secretary Roberto de Ocampo said the government might seek a reconsideration of a Supreme Court order declaring oil industry deregulation unconstitutional. \par \par \uc2  News of the court order dampened the peso earlier this week. De Ocampo said the government was considering delaying its exit from the IMF programme after the Supreme Court decision. The Philippines is scheduled to exit from 35 years of IMF supervision in December. \par \par \uc2  In North Asia, the Korean won came under fire again, ending at new lows of 979.90 to the dollar despite \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar sales at the 975 won and 980 won levels in early trade. \par \par \uc2  Seoul's composite stock index closed at 515.63 points, down a record 6.9 percent. \par \par \uc2  "It's like a ping-pong game. The won gets hit and foreigners dump shares, and thus the won gets hit further," said SEOULBANK dealer Lee Jong-ho. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the won was floundering as financial institutions had difficulty borrowing funds overseas after Moody's and Standard and Poor's credit rating agencies downgraded South Korean banks and debt ratings. \par \par \uc2  Fears Seoul would be forced to turn to the IMF for help as it dealt with a string of corporate defaults and shrinking foreign reserves continued to swirl around the market despite government denials. \par \par \uc2  "I think the won will continue to be weak. There are a lot of internal problems in South Korea. Companies are dealing with liquidity problems and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't have enough reserves to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," a Singapore dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "The pessimism is not necessarily justified but a lot of people are building positions to attack the won. Sentiment is bearish and market is just trying to make up stories," he said. \par \par \uc2  A senior Bank of Korea official denied newspaper reports that its foreign reserves may already be too depleted for a continued defence of the won. \par \par \uc2  Official figures put foreign reserves at $30.5 billion at end October, about 2.5 times Korea's monthly imports. \par \par \uc2  IMF Managing Director Michel Camdessus backed Seoul's efforts to reassure investors, saying South Korea has taken bold steps to reduce external imbalances in recent months. \par \par \uc2  The won's woes depressed the Taiwan dollar as players worried that further depreciation in South Korea -- a key industrial rival -- would undermine Taiwan's export competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar ended at T$30.952 against Thursday's T$30.700 close. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar was little changed at 7.7300/20 to the U.S. dollar from Thursday's 7.7315/25 close. \par \par \uc2  But forwards and interbank rates firmed on fears of a speculative attack even as Camdessus praised Hong Kong's recent defence of its dollar and said the IMF "strongly endorses" the government's commitment to the peg to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtb702ttm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
